+Page 382 (PDF page 396), directly below eq. (21.7) `E(X^p) = x0^p alpha/(alpha - p)`, the text "we can verify that the expectation from Eq. 21.7 is convex to alpha" is followed by the display `d^2 E(X^p)/d alpha^2 = x0^p * 2/(alpha - 1)^3`. Differentiating (21.7) gives `m_p'(alpha) = -p x0^p/(alpha - p)^2` and `m_p''(alpha) = 2 p x0^p/(alpha - p)^3` for `alpha > p`: the printed display omits the factor `p` and replaces `(alpha - p)^3` by `(alpha - 1)^3`, so, as an identity in `alpha`, it agrees with the correct value only for `p = 1`. (For a fixed negative order the two expressions can coincide accidentally at a single `alpha`: `L = 1`, `p = -1/8`, `alpha = 1/4` gives `-128/27` for both; this does not make the printed display a derivative identity. Refinement suggested by the audit of v0.2.9.) Convexity of `m_p` on `alpha > p` does hold for every `p > 0` (the corrected second derivative is positive there); it fails to be inherited by negative orders. Found by the independent audit of v0.2.8 (D028-1) and checked against the PDF during the v0.2.9 pass. The moment identification itself, (21.7), is proved for the actual pinned Pareto law (`AuditPareto.integral_rpow_paretoMeasure`); the convexity and the integrated Jensen statement of Proposition 21.1 are the open part of T118 and must use the corrected derivative.
 
